WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:07.380
And let's look at our second button – "Review translation"

2
00:00:07.570 --> 00:00:11.850
using the same talk as an example. We click it.

3
00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:18.430
Here we can work in

4
00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:26.040
several modes: either editing the text, just as it is here – we click and edit,

5
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:31.760
or we can switch to "subtitles".

6
00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:34.480
The mode for working with subtitles.

7
00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:39.760
You see, here we have two video recordings, and separately for them –

8
00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:42.440
each one has its own subtitles.

9
00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:50.590
So we can switch to working in this mode and edit here.

10
00:00:53.680 --> 00:01:00.390
If we can or need to watch the video at the same time, there is a button

11
00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:01.430
"Show video".

12
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.380
It can be dragged.

13
00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:13.440
And you also see the small "Revert this block edit" buttons.

14
00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:18.420
That is, if you need to undo the edit in this block, we click – and it

15
00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:25.270
is undone. If we made edits in several blocks and then want to reset it all,

16
00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:28.030
we can use the red "Revert all" – and

17
00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:31.590
they will disappear.

18
00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:40.370
When we work in this mode, then automatically,

19
00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:46.690
in step with the video, if we chose to work with the video, the edits

20
00:01:46.690 --> 00:01:52.370
will also move along. Our subtitles will be highlighted as well.

21
00:02:05.830 --> 00:02:12.490
You can see the subtitles running, but unlike the preview mode, here we cannot

22
00:02:12.490 --> 00:02:19.250
go fullscreen with these subtitles. Here our attention is more on

23
00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:24.830
the original and the translation, and on the context around them.

24
00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:31.730
Here we focus more on the text than on listening.

25
00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:38.230
We try whatever suits each of us better.

26
00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:45.950
When our editing is ready – for example, we removed a dot here – then

27
00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:50.210
we can submit these changes.

28
00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:55.980
We have two buttons – an orange one and a green-and-orange one.

29
00:02:56.360 --> 00:03:02.370
This one is in effect for passing it on as a comment, for example,

30
00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:09.150
if you are not sure about the changes and just want

31
00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:12.990
to add your comments, for example...

32
00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:15.210
A comment from the translator:

33
00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:19.930
maybe it would be better to simplify something?

34
00:03:20.470 --> 00:03:26.630
If you just want to pass on a comment in this mode, it's best to click "Create

35
00:03:26.630 --> 00:03:27.630
Issue".

36
00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:34.020
Here you can first preview what it will look like.

37
00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:39.220
Here we will have the suggested edits, that is, what we are proposing.

38
00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:46.400
Maybe it's better to simplify, and remove the dot here. We can create a proposal like this

39
00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:54.910
and record it, so to speak, like this. And then you can discuss it with someone

40
00:03:54.910 --> 00:03:56.830
else, or send it to me.

41
00:03:58.870 --> 00:04:04.300
On the whole, the proposal is recorded, but it still needs more work.

42
00:04:06.430 --> 00:04:13.110
But if we're working in final-edit mode, that is, we understand everything – there shouldn't

43
00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:18.930
be a dot here, and there shouldn't be a dot here – it's a clear-cut edit, and we want to pass it

44
00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:22.170
into the system, there's nothing to discuss here, as they say,

45
00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:28.800
then it's more fitting to click not the orange

46
00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:31.120
"Create Issue", but "Open in GitHub Editor".

47
00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:36.830
What does it tell us? To click "Open GitHub" – this button – and in the editor

48
00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:40.430
select all (Ctrl+A) and paste (Ctrl+V).

49
00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:43.610
We click and try to do it.

50
00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:52.030
Here we put the cursor inside this big block. We can use either

51
00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:58.200
the key combination, or simply "Select all", and

52
00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:06.670
"Select All" if your system interface is in Ukrainian – then paste.

53
00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:15.710
Pasted. After that we click green buttons three times: the first one is here,

54
00:05:15.710 --> 00:05:20.350
at the top right, "Commit changes"; the second, in this dialog, is "Propose

55
00:05:20.350 --> 00:05:21.350
changes",

56
00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:26.990
and the third is "Create pull request".

57
00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:34.900
When you submit changes in this format, they are already

58
00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:37.440
final proposals.

59
00:05:37.460 --> 00:05:44.380
So to speak – that is, inside a specific file, here we see, in

60
00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:50.750
the final subtitles for the 1987 Vishnumaya Puja,

61
00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:57.170
we made two edits, and here a new line also

62
00:05:57.170 --> 00:05:59.710
got removed – that's what needed fixing.

63
00:06:01.070 --> 00:06:06.170
And when we refresh, after a while we'll see that our edits have also been

64
00:06:06.170 --> 00:06:12.880
synced

65
00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:15.820
into the other subtitles and into the transcript.

66
00:06:16.690 --> 00:06:23.630
This way, nobody will need to go and sync by hand the changes that

67
00:06:23.630 --> 00:06:25.510
were proposed in one source.

68
00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:33.130
In the case of this talk, for example, we have three sources – subtitles for the talk, subtitles

69
00:06:33.130 --> 00:06:37.210
for the full recording with the protocol, and the transcript.

70
00:06:38.290 --> 00:06:40.070
So the sync has happened.

71
00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:41.910
Indeed, two dots were removed –

72
00:06:46.810 --> 00:06:53.130
both here and here. And, by the way, that extra change moving text to a new

73
00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:54.130
line is gone.

74
00:06:56.580 --> 00:06:59.140
So, at this stage...

75
00:06:59.270 --> 00:07:02.810
But still, something didn't go quite as it should...

76
00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:11.300
At this stage your work of submitting changes is done. You can take this

77
00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:17.840
link or the number of this proposal, in this case 487,

78
00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:24.360
and send it to me, so that I can take another look

79
00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:29.640
at this edit with a second, technical eye: whether all the automations worked here.

80
00:07:29.660 --> 00:07:36.600
In this case they didn't, and that's exactly what needs looking into. And then I just click this

81
00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:43.090
button, and the changes will be in the main version. And here, you see,

82
00:07:43.770 --> 00:07:46.740
is your separate version.

83
00:07:48.020 --> 00:07:54.890
It will be named after you – "name-patch-4". In fact, it can be

84
00:07:54.890 --> 00:07:59.650
customised: when we click the green buttons, you can customise it there if

85
00:07:59.650 --> 00:08:01.790
needed, but by default it's in this format.

86
00:08:06.030 --> 00:08:07.030
And

87
00:08:09.070 --> 00:08:15.670
this same version can be used on the portal, right here. So let's

88
00:08:15.730 --> 00:08:18.730
try to find a use for it, shall we?

89
00:08:19.410 --> 00:08:26.150
In fact, if you have this proposal with such a version, and it has a great many edits,

90
00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:30.050
and you tell someone, "Please,

91
00:08:31.230 --> 00:08:34.570
look over this proposed version of mine,

92
00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:42.320
because there are many edits, maybe something still needs fixing, and please don't

93
00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:47.520
replace the main version with my edits yet."

94
00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:54.100
Then you can take this version, the one named after you.

95
00:08:56.420 --> 00:09:02.850
On this portal, let's revert all the changes, as if they never happened – the dots

96
00:09:02.850 --> 00:09:04.190
are back in place.

97
00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:12.160
Now we can refresh, to make sure that

98
00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:14.300
our edit is here.

99
00:09:15.450 --> 00:09:20.020
And we choose – let's find "SlavaSubotskiy-patch-4".

100
00:09:22.050 --> 00:09:27.820
We click it and get the version without the dots here.

101
00:09:28.460 --> 00:09:35.320
So in this way, by working like this, we can do

102
00:09:35.320 --> 00:09:42.280
an extra review of the changed version, the proposed version, not only

103
00:09:42.280 --> 00:09:43.420
on your computer.

104
00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:46.020
That is, at first your edits were only on your computer.

105
00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:53.500
But once you have submitted them in this format, when a special version is created, your

106
00:09:53.500 --> 00:09:57.780
named one, you can pass it to someone to review.

107
00:09:57.780 --> 00:10:01.940
Besides, you can take this very link.

108
00:10:02.980 --> 00:10:05.360
If you have switched to your version,

109
00:10:06.390 --> 00:10:13.190
it will contain your named version and this very talk you are

110
00:10:13.190 --> 00:10:19.570
watching. Anyone who opens it via this link will land right on

111
00:10:19.570 --> 00:10:26.070
your special named version and will be able to

112
00:10:26.070 --> 00:10:33.060
finish proofreading it. Well, that's all about working

113
00:10:33.060 --> 00:10:39.750
with the review page. That's "Review

114
00:10:39.750 --> 00:10:40.750
translation".
